## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated
against the delta time the game is given, so an interval of game time reaches
the same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make it
driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is
  handed, independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of
  wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing
  from the renderer. Whether a swap makes a match, and what a chain step clears,
  is decided from the board alone, never from anything the renderer holds.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded
  from `state.rngState`, and it keeps its whole generator state in that field,
  so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same result exactly.
  The deal of an opening board and the refill in R9 draw from it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the
game reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath
the game, and the surface carries no operation for the registered actions or for
the overlay. That layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key
bindings this specification fixes, it reports the pointer position in the
stage's logical units along with the press and release edges, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it reports the pointer position in the
stage's logical units along with the press and release edges, and it draws and
toggles the debug overlay.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the pointer, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it hands the game the pointer's ordered
samples in the stage's logical units with the press and release edges, and it
draws and toggles the debug overlay.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Facet's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`FACET_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans. `x` and
`y` are in the stage's logical units, `col` and `row` are cell coordinates as
`specs/board.md` defines them, and every duration is game time in seconds.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<FacetState>`. A pose returns the next `FacetState`, built from the
one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read.
The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{else}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its heading names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall clock, so the game changes
only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to running itself,
which is how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left.

### `advance(seconds, frames)`

Runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game time, each worth
`seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults to `1`. Each is
a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render, so the game's
own chain cadence and end conditions produce the result and the canvas reflects
it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's delta
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 60)` cover the same second of game time
and must reach the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size
explains.

Advancing while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `reset(options)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value: the
title screen with its first menu item highlighted, no board in play, `phase`
`idle` with `chainStep` and `stepTimer` at `0`, `score` at `0`, `level` at `1`,
`levelScore` at `0`, `lastCleared` and `lastPoints` at `0`, the cursor at
`(0, 0)`, no selection and no refusal, the pointer reported as up, and `simTime`
at `0`. `options.seed` seeds `rngState`, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`).

`muted` is untouched; the runtime owns muting.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `start()`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `start(state)`
{{else}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `start()`
{{/if}}

Poses the choice of `PLAY` from the title menu, which is the same choice
`PLAY AGAIN` makes from `gameover`. A round begins: `score` at `0`, `level` at
`1`, `levelScore` at `0`, `phase` `idle` with `chainStep` at `0`, `menuIndex`
at `0`, the cursor at `(0, 0)`, no selection, no refusal, and a fresh opening
board dealt through the game's own code, so it holds no run under R4 and carries
at least one legal swap, as `specs/rules.md` requires. The screen becomes
`playing`.

It leaves `rngState`, `simTime`, and `muted` as they stand, so a round from a
known deal is a `reset` carrying a seed followed by this.

