{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `quit()`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `quit(state)`
{{else}}
### `quit()`
{{/if}}

Poses the choice of `QUIT`, which the pause menu and the game-over menu both
offer. The round ends: the screen becomes `title` with its first menu item
highlighted, no board is in play, `phase` is `idle` with `chainStep` and
`stepTimer` at `0`, and there is no selection and no refusal. `score`, `level`,
and `levelScore` hold what the round left them at, and the next `start` begins a
fresh round.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `loadBoard(rows)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `loadBoard(state, rows)`
{{else}}
### `loadBoard(rows)`
{{/if}}

Poses an arbitrary board and moves to `playing`, with `phase` `idle`,
`chainStep` and `stepTimer` at `0`, no selection, and no refusal. `rows` is the
board notation `specs/board.md` defines, one string per row, `GRID_ROWS` (`8`)
of them carrying `GRID_COLS` (`8`) tokens each. Cell centers follow the formulas
in `specs/board.md`, so a posed board sits on the stage exactly where a dealt
board does. `score`, `level`, `levelScore`, `rngState`, and `simTime` stand
where they were.

A board posed this way is a board like any other, and the rules in
`specs/rules.md` govern it unchanged. Those rules run a chain from an accepted
swap and evaluate the level and end conditions when one settles, so a posed
board rests exactly as it was written until a swap is accepted on it.

