{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `requestSwap(colA, rowA, colB, rowB)`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `requestSwap(state, colA, rowA, colB, rowB)`
{{else}}
### `requestSwap(colA, rowA, colB, rowB)`
{{/if}}

Requests the swap of `(colA, rowA)` with `(colB, rowB)` through the same
acceptance path a player's swap takes, so R1, R2, and R3 in `specs/rules.md`
decide it and nothing is bypassed. An accepted swap exchanges the two cells and
resolves its first chain step on the spot, exactly as `specs/rules.md`
describes. A refused swap leaves the board as it stands and names the two cells
as the standing refusal for `REFUSAL_SECONDS` (`0.3`).

It names both cells itself, so the selection stands where it was either way.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()`
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
### `pointerDown(state, x, y)`, `pointerMove(state, x, y)`, `pointerUp(state)`
{{else}}
### `pointerDown(x, y)`, `pointerMove(x, y)`, `pointerUp()`
{{/if}}

Report a press, a move, and a release at a logical stage position. They feed the
same input path a real pointer feeds, so a posed press and a player's press are
the same event to the game: a press resolves against the cell whose center is
within `GEM_HIT_R` (`36`) of `(x, y)` by exactly the press rules in
`specs/controls.md`, a drag from a cell to an orthogonally adjacent one requests
that swap under those same rules, and a swap so requested is decided by R1, R2,
and R3 in `specs/rules.md` like any other.

Each of the three **takes effect immediately**, when it is called, rather than
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
being sampled once per update. The press, the move, or the release is resolved
in the state the call returns rather than deferred to the next update. No frame
need pass between them, so a selection and the swap it leads to can both be
posed without advancing the game at all.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
being sampled once per update. The press, the move, or the release is resolved
in the state the call returns rather than deferred to the next update. Each
returns the state that press, move, or release left behind, and the next call is
handed that state, so a selection and the swap it leads to can both be posed
without a frame passing between the calls.
{{else}}
being sampled once per frame. The press, the move, or the release is resolved
against the live state before the call returns rather than deferred to the next
frame, through the same per-sample resolution the player controller feeds each
frame. No frame need pass between them, so a selection and the swap it leads to
can both be posed without advancing the game at all.
{{/if}}

A press farther than `GEM_HIT_R` from every cell center leaves the board and the
selection as they stand.

