## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:
{{else}}
## Snapshot shape

`snapshot()` returns:
{{/if}}

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover",
  menuIndex: <number>,        // the screen's highlighted menu item, from 0
  score: <number>,
  level: <number>,            // the level being played, counted from 1
  levelScore: <number>,       // banked toward the current level
  levelTarget: <number>,      // LEVEL_TARGET_STEP * level, derived
  phase: "idle" | "resolving",
  chainStep: <number>,        // 0 while idle
  multiplier: <number>,       // min(chainStep, MAX_MULTIPLIER), derived
  stepTimer: <number>,        // game time accumulated toward the next step
  board: {
    cols: <number>,
    rows: <number>,
    cells: [
      { col, row, x, y,
        kind: <string | null>,
        cut: "plain" | "brilliant" | "star" | "prism",
        strain: <number> },
    ],
  },
  cursor: { col: <number>, row: <number> },
  selection: { col: <number>, row: <number> } | null,
  refusal: { a: { col, row }, b: { col, row } } | null,
  lastCleared: <number>,      // cells cleared by the most recent chain step
  lastPoints: <number>,       // points the most recent chain step scored
  legalSwap: <boolean>,       // whether any legal swap exists, derived
  rngState: <number>,
  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>, down: <boolean> },
  muted: <boolean>,
  simTime: <number>,          // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present on every screen. A field with
nothing to report holds its resting value rather than going missing: `board` is
`{ cols: 0, rows: 0, cells: [] }` while no board is in play, `selection` and
`refusal` are `null` while none stands, `chainStep` and `stepTimer` are `0`
while `phase` is `idle`, and `menuIndex` is live on every screen carrying a menu
and rests at `0` on `playing`. `cells` lists every cell of the board in reading
order from the top-left cell to the bottom-right, and a cell's `kind` is `null`
for a `prism`.

Every field is read straight off the game's state, so what the snapshot reports
is what the game holds. Four are derived rather than stored:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| a cell's `x`, `y` | the cell center formulas in `specs/board.md` |
| `levelTarget` | `LEVEL_TARGET_STEP` (`2000`) times `level` |
| `multiplier` | `min(chainStep, MAX_MULTIPLIER)`, `MAX_MULTIPLIER` (`8`) |
| `legalSwap` | R1 and R3 in `specs/rules.md`, over the board as it stands |

Three fields need the game to keep them honest every frame:

- `pointer` mirrors the position the pointer input reports and whether the
  pointer is currently pressed, refreshed in every update.
- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every update, since the
  runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of it.
- `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every update, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least the current `screen` and `phase`, the
board's `cols` and `rows`, the score, the level and the level score against the
level's target, the chain step and the multiplier, how many cells the most
recent chain step cleared and what it scored, the cursor cell and the selected
cell, whether a legal swap exists, and the pointer position, the same facts the
snapshot reports. Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every
source a pure read, so watching the overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Facet's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<FacetState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Facet's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
